I've written the plan as two task files plus planned issue replies in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing because .gitignore excludes session_plan/, so the files exist on disk but aren't committed (the plan's || true allows for that). I haven't checked whether the harness reads them from disk.

Task 1: Part of #1086 (product). On the shell, 11 subcommands still print their REPL slash form in usage and error text (for example yoyo run prints usage: /run <command>  or  !<command>). This task adds one shared helper that returns /verb in the REPL and yoyo verb on the shell, next to the existing DoctorSurface enum in src/commands_dev.rs, and wires yoyo run into it. After this, each remaining verb should be a one-file fix.
• Surface passed as an argument, not a global flag: dispatch_sub.rs's own tests dispatch subcommands in-process, so a global would leak "shell" into REPL tests.
• Size gate: commands_dev.rs has about 35 lines left under its 2000-line cap. dispatch_sub.rs is 24 lines over its register entry but still inside the 50-line allowance.
• Afterwards: the task re-runs the #1086 probe and posts the new count on the ticket. Expected 10 of 11; 10 verbs and the yoyo help doctor topic will still be left.

Task 2: Fixes #1068 (product, the backlog-drain slot). I picked this one as the open issue with the least work left. Its first defect already shipped in dc11ae62. The only open item is a doc in src/hard_deny.rs that claims too much. It says a quoted string is one word. But the command is split into lines first, so lines inside a multi-line quoted argument (like python3 -c "…") are checked as commands. This only ever refuses too much, never too little.
• The change: correct that sentence, add it to the module's list of known gaps, and add a test that pins the multi-line refusal next to a single-line case that must still pass.
• Fallback: if the multi-line case turns out not to be refused at HEAD, the gap goes the other way (it lets the command through). The task then lands as "Part of #1068" instead.

